  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote the tower-stacking game in `src/main.ts`. It typechecks, but I couldn't run `vite build` or open it in a browser, so none of it has been run or seen on screen.\n\n- **Scene:** Aura3D with a fixed pool of box slabs (one per level) and a matching pool of cut-off pieces. They are moved and scaled each frame through runtime node handles. Slab color shifts in hue by level.\n- **Gameplay:**\n  - Space, a click or a tap on the canvas drops the slab. A perfect drop (within 0.12 of the slab below) keeps the width and adds a bonus of 1.\n  - Otherwise the overhang is cut and falls away. If the slab misses entirely, the whole slab falls and the status becomes `lost`.\n  - The slab speeds up every 3 levels. Reaching 25 stacked slabs sets `won`.\n  - R, or a click on the canvas after the game ends, restarts with a fresh base slab.\n- **Camera:** I scroll the world down as the tower grows instead of moving the camera, so the top stays in view.\n- **HUD and state:** The DOM HUD below the canvas shows score, best for the session and status, plus a short \"perfect\" message. `window.__TASK_STATE__` is updated after every change. `window.__TASK_READY__` is set after the first frames.\n- **Canvas and top-level await:** I passed `resize: false` to `createAuraApp` so the canvas stays 640x360. I avoided top-level `await` so `vite build`'s default target can handle it.\n\nThe task didn't say whether the base slab counts toward `height`. I chose not to count it, so `height` is the number of slabs successfully stacked on the base (0 after a restart), and `won` happens at `height` 25.\n\n",
